### What does enable.auto.commit do?

The `enable.auto.commit` setting, default true, makes the consumer commit the last polled offsets automatically every `auto.commit.interval.ms`, by default five seconds. The problem is that this timer is independent of whether you have finished processing, so auto-commit can advance the offset past records you have not yet handled.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class AutoCommitConfig {
    // WRONG: auto-commit risks skipping unprocessed records
    public static Properties dangerousConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_COMMIT_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "5000");
        System.out.println("WRONG: auto-commit can lose records on crash");
        return props;
    }

    // CORRECT: manual commit for at-least-once
    public static Properties safeConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");
        System.out.println("CORRECT: manual commit after processing");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        dangerousConfig();
        safeConfig();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: auto-commit can lose records on crash
CORRECT: manual commit after processing
```

### What does auto.offset.reset do?

The `auto.offset.reset` setting decides where to start only when there is no valid committed offset for the group and partition, which happens for a brand-new group or when the committed offset has been deleted by retention.

| Value | Behavior | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| `earliest` | Start at oldest available record | Process full history |
| `latest` | Start at end, skip existing | Only new messages |
| `none` | Throw exception | Fail loudly rather than guess |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class OffsetResetDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "new-fraud-scorer");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());

        // earliest: new group reads ALL existing transactions
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "earliest");
        System.out.println("New group 'new-fraud-scorer' will read entire backlog");

        // latest would skip everything, which is a classic cause of
        // "my new consumer appears to receive nothing"

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList("transfers"));
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
            System.out.println("Received " + records.count() + " backlog records");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
New group 'new-fraud-scorer' will read entire backlog
Received 45000 backlog records
```

### What does max.poll.records do?

The `max.poll.records` setting, default 500, caps how many records a single call to `poll()` returns. It is not a total limit on consumption and not a byte limit; it just shapes each batch. You tune it down when each record is expensive to process.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class MaxPollRecordsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // For lightweight records: larger batches for throughput
        Properties highThroughput = new Properties();
        highThroughput.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, "1000");
        System.out.println("High throughput: 1000 records per poll");

        // For expensive fraud scoring: smaller batches to finish within poll interval
        Properties expensiveProcessing = new Properties();
        expensiveProcessing.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, "50");
        System.out.println("Expensive processing: 50 records per poll");
        System.out.println("  Ensures batch completes within max.poll.interval.ms");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
High throughput: 1000 records per poll
Expensive processing: 50 records per poll
  Ensures batch completes within max.poll.interval.ms
```

### What does max.poll.interval.ms do?

The `max.poll.interval.ms` setting, default five minutes, bounds the time allowed between two successive `poll()` calls. If your handler takes longer than this to process a batch, the group coordinator assumes the consumer is stuck, removes it from the group, and triggers a rebalance.

This is why a slow message handler causes a rebalance even when heartbeats are still flowing: the heartbeat thread proves the process is up, while the poll interval proves processing is progressing.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class PollIntervalDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "fraud-scorer");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, "50");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "300000"); // 5 minutes

        System.out.println("Config: 50 records per poll, 5 min poll interval");
        System.out.println("Each record must be scored in < 6 seconds to stay safe");
        // 50 records * 6 sec each = 300 sec = 5 min poll interval
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Config: 50 records per poll, 5 min poll interval
Each record must be scored in < 6 seconds to stay safe
```

### How do these four settings interact?

Manual commit plus a right-sized `max.poll.records` plus a sane `max.poll.interval.ms` together give a consumer that is both safe and stable: it processes bounded batches, commits only after success, and does not get evicted mid-batch.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class RecommendedConsumerConfig {
    public static Properties create() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");

        // Safe commit: manual after processing
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");

        // Start from beginning for new group
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "earliest");

        // Bounded batches for expensive processing
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, "100");

        // Generous but bounded poll interval
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS_CONFIG, "300000");

        System.out.println("Recommended config: safe, complete, and stable");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        create();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Recommended config: safe, complete, and stable
```

### What is the auto.offset.reset=none option?

Setting `auto.offset.reset=none` throws a `NoOffsetForPartitionException` if there is no committed offset, which you use when you want to fail loudly rather than silently guess where to start.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class OffsetResetNoneDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "strict-audit-group");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "none");

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList("transfers"));
            consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
        } catch (org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.NoOffsetForPartitionException e) {
            System.out.println("ERROR: No committed offset found for group");
            System.out.println("Use 'none' to fail loudly rather than guess");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
ERROR: No committed offset found for group
Use 'none' to fail loudly rather than guess
```

### What are common mistakes with consumer configs?

The most common mistake is leaving auto-commit enabled and assuming at-least-once delivery. Another frequent error is setting `auto.offset.reset=latest` for a new group and then wondering why the consumer appears to receive nothing, because it skipped the entire backlog. A third mistake is setting `max.poll.records` very high without raising `max.poll.interval.ms`, causing the consumer to be evicted mid-batch.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class ConsumerConfigMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: auto-commit + at-least-once claim");
        System.out.println("  enable.auto.commit=true (default)");
        System.out.println("  Crash between poll and commit -> records lost");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: latest for a new group that needs history");
        System.out.println("  auto.offset.reset=latest");
        System.out.println("  New group skips entire backlog silently");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: large batch + short poll interval");
        System.out.println("  max.poll.records=5000, max.poll.interval.ms=30000");
        System.out.println("  Expensive processing -> eviction -> rebalance storm");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: manual commit, earliest, sized batches");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: auto-commit + at-least-once claim
  enable.auto.commit=true (default)
  Crash between poll and commit -> records lost

WRONG: latest for a new group that needs history
  auto.offset.reset=latest
  New group skips entire backlog silently

WRONG: large batch + short poll interval
  max.poll.records=5000, max.poll.interval.ms=30000
  Expensive processing -> eviction -> rebalance storm

CORRECT: manual commit, earliest, sized batches
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

After a rebalance caused by a blown poll interval, the reassigned partitions may be reprocessed, so idempotency still matters. When the setting does nothing: `auto.offset.reset` is irrelevant when a committed offset already exists, because the consumer simply resumes there. A common misconception is that `max.poll.records` limits total consumption; it only limits per-poll batch size.
